1	# The roadmap format — definition
2	
3	This page defines the format the roadmap is written in. The roadmap is a member of the same format family as the spec, and this page states only what is particular to the roadmap. The shared family laws live once in `docs/spec-format.md`, and this page inherits them by reference rather than restating them.
4	
5	## What the roadmap inherits from the family
6	
7	The roadmap is written in the requirements genre `docs/spec-format.md` defines, and every law that page states holds here unchanged:
8	
9	- the closed-vocabulary glossary, so every domain noun the roadmap uses carries one glossary entry under one name;
10	- the keyword form, so the keywords *when*, *while*, *if*, *then*, and *shall* are set in lowercase italics;
11	- the no-capitals rule, so no word stands in all capitals outside a code anchor;
12	- the trailing code anchor, so a code points to its home at the line's end and a reader may ignore it;
13	- the no-history law, so the record of finished work — its dates, its delivery reports, the reasons behind past choices — lives in the archive and the journal, and the body states only the live queue;
14	- the generated-section gating, so a section a script builds is output only and a gate reds a hand edit of it (to red is to fail the suite), where the roadmap carries such a section;
15	- the comprehension gate, so a changed section clears the mechanical lints and then a panel of cold readers, passing only after two consecutive reads return zero blocking findings.
16	
17	A reader who needs any of these consults `docs/spec-format.md`. The rest of this page adds the parts the roadmap carries that the spec does not.
